## INTRODUCTION
In the past few years, exosomes as the emerging and promising biomarkers have attracted much attention in early cancer diagnosis.1,2 Exosomes are nano-sized phospholipid bilayer extracellular vesicles with main size distribution of $30{\mathrm{-}}100\ \mathrm{nm}$ . They can be secreted by various cells, such as leukocytes, cancerous and epithelial cells. Exosomes exist in biological fluids, including blood, urine, breast milk and saliva of human bodies.3,4 Exosomes are released from multivesicular bodies via endolysosomal pathway and carry many macromolecules from parental cells, such as transmembrane and cytoplasmic proteins, mRNA, DNA and micro RNA.4 Exosomes serve as the messengers of mediating intercellular communication to indicate diseases and the cancer-related alteration of physiological status, showing the role as biomarkers. Detection of exosomes can be applied to early cancer diagnosis, which overcomes some challenges in traditional cancer detection, such as invasive screening, high cost and low sensitivity.5,6 So far, previous studies have involved exosomes detection,7–18 but the selective and sensitive detection of exosomes is still challenging. One challenge is direct and specific analysis of nanoscale exosomes. Flow cytometry has the limited detection efficiency due to weak light scattering, while nanoparticle-tracking analysis (NTA) is lack of specificity during exosomes detection. Another challenge is sensitive detection of exosomes in low concentrations. In the early stage of cancers, the sensitive detection of low-concentration exosomes is required for early cancer screening and diagnosis through non-invasive pathways.
Different analysis methods were employed to detect exosomes, such as surface-enhanced Raman scattering,12,19 surface plasmon resonance,20,21 electrochemiluminescence,14,22 colorimetry,8,9,11 upconversion luminescence,7,15 fluorescence,13,18,23 and electrochemical detection.4,10,17,24,25 Among analysis methods of exosomes, aptamer-based electrochemical biosensors (aptasensors) attract much attention.4,10 Electrochemical aptasensors have some intrinsic merits, including specific recognition of targets with DNA aptamers, rapid response, excellent portability, high sensitivity and moderate requirements of sample volumes.4,26–31 In contrast to other analysis methods, aptasensors exhibit excellent specificity and high affinity of DNA aptamers generated from the in-vitro selection. Aptasensors of exosomes have been developed, but most of them use a single-signal output to detect exosomes.4,7,8,10,14,18 This output mode depends on accurate measurements of single-signal intensities, which are difficult to perform in the sensing systems due to inevitable effects of intrinsic or extrinsic factors, such as the contents of sensing materials, instrument errors, background signals and the fluctuation of environmental conditions. Except for a response signal, the introduction of a reference signal can form an intrinsic self-calibration sensing platform with dual-signal output.
Dual-signal self-calibration sensing platform exhibits the improved stability, high reproducibility and reliability in practical detection applications.32–40
Previous studies reported dual-signal detection of exosomes. Zhang et al. designed an electrochemical biosensor based on bipedal DNA walkers for attomolar detection of exosomal miR-21.41 Xia et al. developed a fluorescence bioprobe based on DNA-labeled carbon dots coupling with target-catalyzing signal amplification for detection of exosomal miR-21.42 Luo et al. constructed a DNA biosensor based on a locked nucleic acid-modified “Y” shapelike structure for detection of exosomal miR-21.43 Zhang et al. explored a $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXenes-based platform based on fluorescence Resonance energy transfer for exosomes detection.23 Zhao et al. reported a target-triggered 3D DNA walking machine and exonuclease III-assisted biosensing of exosomes.44 As for exosomes detection, these studies involve complex detection mechanisms and tedious steps to fabricate the sensing platforms, causing the weakened detection stability, low reproducibility and poor reliability. So far, new analysis techniques for efficient detection of exosomes are required and can promote early cancer diagnosis in clinical. Herein, black phosphorous nanosheets (BPNSs) were facilely assembled with ferrocene (Fc)-doped metal-organic frameworks (MOF, ZIF-67) on indium tin oxide (ITO) to prepare functional hybrid thin-film, followed by combining methylene blue (MB)-labeled singlestrand DNA (ssDNA) aptamer with ITO to form a sensing platform of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO (Scheme 1). As a smart aptasensor, the novel platform enabled precise protein capture, dual-signal and intrinsic self-calibration sensing of cancer-derived exosomes.
As one of specific proteins carried by exosomes, CD63 transmembrane protein is relative to membrane transport, invasion and fusion.7–9 The precise capture of CD63 protein facilitates the detection of exosomes. CD63 protein is abundantly expressed on MCF-7 cell-secreted exosomes. Aptamer of CD63 protein can be used as the capture and recognition probe.20–25,44 In this work, MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer was prepared and enabled the capture of CD63 protein through specific target-aptamer interactions. The capture process reveals the combination of aptamer with MCF-7 cell-secreted exosomes. Efficient detection of exosomes indicates breast cancer MCF-7 cells existing in biological fluids of human bodies. This detection technique can be used in clinical assays for the early diagnosis of breast cancers. As for the sensing platform (Scheme 1), Fc as a reference was doped into ZIF-67 and had negligible signal changes after addition of exosomes. However, MB as a response probe had obvious signal changes, because specific combination of MB-labeled aptamer with exosomes caused the aptamer away from ITO and resulted in a decreased redox peak current of MB. An intrinsic self-calibration aptasensor was achieved based on the platform, using the dual-signal ratio of redox peak current intensities $(\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{MB}})$ as signal output. Due to attractive merits such as facile process, intrinsic self-calibration, specificity, high sensitivity, rapid response and low detection limit, this aptasensor can be expanded to multiple biomarkers from cell line exosomes and is beneficial for efficient detection of cancer-derived exosomes and early cancer screening and diagnosis in clinical.
