## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemicals. Exosomes secreted by breast cancer MCF-7 cells were purchased from Shanghai Gefan Biotech Co., Ltd., China. MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer for CD63 protein with base sequences of $5'$ -MB- $(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{5}$ -TTT TTT CAC CCC ACC TCG CTC CCG TGA CAC TAA TGC TA- $.3^{\circ}$ and a random aptamer with sequences of $5^{\circ}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -TTT TTT ACA CAT TAC AGG GGT TCG GTC TGG AAA GCA GTT ACT GTC CCC TTG GGT- $.3^{\circ}$ were prepared and purified by Shanghai Sangon Biotech Co., Ltd., China. $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ $(98\%)$ , uric acid (UA), ascorbic acid (AA), dopamine (DA), 2-methylimidazole $(98\%)$ , glucose, bovine serum albumin (BSA), cysteine, glutathione, Fc, MB and cholesterol were provided by Sigma-Aldrich. NaCl, KCl, $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ , ethanol, N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) and other chemicals with the analytical grade were bought from Shanghai Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China. Black phosphorus crystals were purchased from Nanjing XFNANO Technology Co., China. All chemicals can be used as received without purification. Doubly distilled water (DDW) was used in experiments and phosphate
buffered saline (PBS) was used for pH adjustments.
Apparatus. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were measured by using a JEM–2100 TEM (JEOL, Japan) at an acceleration voltage of $120\mathrm{kV}$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images was recorded on a JSM– 7401 field emission SEM system (JEOL, Japan). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were measured with a D5005 X-ray powder diffractometer (Siemens, Germany) with the graphite mono-chromatized Cu $K_{a}$ radiation. Fourier transform infrared (FT–IR) spectroscopy was recorded on an iS50 FT–IR (Thermo Nicolet, USA) spectrometer with KBr window in the transmission mode. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was measured with a Kratos XSAM 800 XPS system (Thermo Scientific, USA). UV–vis absorption spectra were recorded by using a UV–2700 spectrophotometer (Shimadzu, Japan). Raman spectra were measured by using a DXR2 laser confocal microscopic Raman spectrometer (Thermo Scientific, USA). CHI-660E electrochemical workstation (Chenhua, China) was used to measure cyclic voltammetry (CV) and square wave voltammetry (SWV) curves. PMC2000 electrochemical workstation (Princeton Applied Research, USA) was used to measure electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) curves. A three-electrode system was equipped for electrochemical measurements, using the bare or modified ITO as working electrode (WE), Ag/AgCl as reference electrode (RE) and $\mathrm{Pt}$ wire as counter electrode (CE). The result was expressed as the average of six repeated measurements.
Preparation of Aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO Platform. Black phosphorus crystals ( $\mathrm{20~mg}$ were dispersed in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of NMP under stirring. The suspension was transferred into an ultrasonic cleaner for sonication (50 W, 3 h), followed by placing in a probe ultrasonic generator for sonication circles (3 min interval). The resultant suspension was centrifuged at $5000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $15~\mathrm{{min}}$ to remove large-sized products. The supernatant was centrifuged at 12000 rpm for $15~\mathrm{{min}}$ to obtain products. After washing with ethanol and natural drying, the products were dispersed in ethanol to prepare the suspension of BPNSs for subsequent use. A commercial ITO slice was washed with ethanol and DDW to gain clean surface. Clean ITO as WE was immersed in homogeneous electrolyte mixture containing $38~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol, 2 mL DDW, 0.1 M $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $0.8\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{~.~}$ 2-methylimidazole and $0.1\ \mathrm{g\Fc}$ . Through CV scan for 200 s at a constant voltage of $-5\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. Ag/AgCl as RE, Fc/ZIF-67 film was electrodeposited on ITO. ZIF-67/ITO film was prepared by the method without Fc. After natural drying of Fc/ZIF-67/ITO, BPNSs suspension was dropcasted to form BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 film on ITO, followed by natural drying. MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer $(0.8~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ dispersed in deoxygenated PBS (1 mM, pH 7.4) was incubated in water bath for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Then, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of aptamer solution was drop-casted on BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO. After incubation for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ ITO sensing platform was constructed for electrochemical measurements.
Construction of Intrinsic Self-Calibration Aptasensor of Exosomes. CV, EIS and SWV electrochemical curves of ITO modified with ZIF-67, Fc/ZIF-67, BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 and aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 were measured under different conditions including aptamer contents, incubation temperature, time and $\mathfrak{p H}$ . As for CV and EIS curves, the bare or modified ITO was immersed in PBS (1 mM, pH 7.4) containing 0.1 M KCl and 1 mM $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4-}}$ as signal probe. SWV curves of modified ITO were measured under various conditions. Under optimal conditions, SWV curves of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO were measured in PBS electrolyte with addition of exosomes. The concentration of exosomes $(C_{\mathrm{exo}})$ was adjusted from 0 to $2.6\times10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{\mL^{-1}}$ . Redox peak current intensities of Fc and MB were converted into a ratio of ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ as signal output. By plotting a linear relationship of ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ versus $c_{\mathrm{exo}},$ an intrinsic self-calibration aptasensor was achieved for exosomes detection.
Selectivity and Detection Assays of this Aptasensor in Practical Samples. Under optimal conditions, aptamerBPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO platform was used for electrochemical measurements in the presence of exosomes ( $1.0{\times}10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) and each interference $\mathrm{(Na^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ , UA, AA, glucose, BSA, cysteine, DA, cholesterol and glutathione) at $1~\mathrm{mM}$ . Peak current intensities $\left({\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}\right)$ measured from SWV curves in the presence of exosomes and interferences were compared to evaluate the selectivity and sensitivity of this aptasensor.
To further study the selectivity, this aptasensor was used to detect exosomes derived from cancer cells, including breast cancer cell line (MCF-7 cells), liver cancer cell line (HepG2 cells) and cervical cancer cell line (HeLa cells) at the same amount ( $1.0{\times}10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ). The cell culture, exosomes extraction, counting and characterization assays were conducted by Shanghai Gefan Biotech Co., Ltd., China. Our group brought the samples of exosomes secreted by different cancer cells. SWV curves of mixture solutions with addition of cancer-derived exosomes were measured. ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ values were compared to verify the selectivity of this aptasensor towards MCF-7 cell-derived exosomes. To test interactions of specific aptamer with CD63 protein, a random aptamer was used to replace the MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer. SWV curves of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO were measured in culture medium, culture supernatant and supernatant filtrate of MCF-7 cells. ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ values were compared to testify the specificity of CD63 protein towards MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer surpassing a random aptamer. The results proved precise capture for CD63 protein based on the use of specific aptamer.
Practicability was studied by detecting exosomes in practical samples. Fresh human serum was collected from a healthy young volunteer in a local hospital of Qingdao, China. Serum samples were 10-fold diluted with PBS (1 mM, pH 7.4), followed by addition of exosomes with each increment of $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ . A series of mixture solutions were prepared by the standard addition method. Aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO was immersed in mixture solutions and SWV curves were measured. $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ values were calculated by a plotted linear relationship between ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ and $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ Human plasma samples collected from three healthy individuals and three breast cancer patients were provided by a local hospital of Qingdao, China. Plasma samples were 10-fold diluted with PBS (1 mM, pH 7.4) to prepare a series of mixture solutions. SWV curves of mixture solutions were measured and $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ values were calculated by the plotted linear relationship between ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ and $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ . $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ values were compared to testify the specific recognition capability of this aptasensor towards breast cancer-derived exosomes in potential patients. The results were used to verify the practicability of this aptasensor for early cancer screening and diagnosis in clinical.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Morphologic, Microstructural and Spectral Characterizations. TEM images were used to test the morphology of exosomes derived from MCF-7 cells. Exosomes exhibit a phospholipid bilayer structure of extracellular vesicles (Figure 1a) and have an average diameter of ${\sim}75~\mathrm{nm}$ , consistent with the diameter range of other cancer-derived exosomes.7,10,20,21 In Figure 1b, BPNSs prepared by liquid-phase sonication exfoliation of black phosphorus crystals show typical stacking and nanosheet-like structures with length or width from tens to hundreds of nanometers.45,46 SEM images (Figure 1c) show the surface of Fc/ZIF-67 film on ITO substrate. After local magnification, Figure 1d displays continuous and nearly uniform morphologies on film surface, implying the growth of Fc/ZIF-67 on ITO.47 Figure 1e presents the microstructures of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 hybrid film on ITO. Nanosheet-like morphologies of BPNSs are observable on hybrid film (Figure 1f). In Figure 1c,e,f, some cracks happened during the natural drying process of products, which do not disturb the characterization of morphologies and microstructures on hybrid film surface. The cross-sectional views ( $\cdot\ 45^{\circ}$ tilt angle) of SEM imaging illustrate the morphologies of Fc/ZIF-67/ITO (Figure 1g) and BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO (Figure 1h). Thicknesses of Fc/ZIF-67 film and BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 hybrid film are approximately $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and $10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively. The results demonstrate the preparation of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 hybrid thin-film on ITO substrate.
Powder XRD patterns were characterized to reveal the crystalline structures of products. XRD patterns of ZIF-67 (Figure 2a) match with previous reports and the standard simulated XRD patterns of ZIF-67 crystals, indicating the preparation of ZIF-67.47–50 XRD patterns of Fc/ZIF-67 are almost consistent with those of ZIF-67, which indicate the doping of Fc into Co-MOF matrix of ZIF-67 with negligible influence on the crystalline structures of ZIF-67.27 As for XRD patterns of BPNSs, two reflective patterned peaks at $2\uptheta=15.9^{\circ}$ and $33.2^{\circ}$ are assigned to the (020) and (040) diffractions, which are consistent with the standard orthorhombic black phosphorus (JCPDS No.73-1358).46 After drop-casting BPNSs on Fc/ZIF-67, XRD patterns of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 retain the main crystalline structures of ZIF-67. FT–IR spectra of products were measured. In Figure 2b, a sharp characteristic band of Co–N appears at $425~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . There is a strong stretching band of $\mathrm{C=N}$ at $1600{-}1650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Blending vibrations of alkane C–H appear at $1450\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .48 After the doping of Fc and drop-casting of BPNSs, FT–IR spectra of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 show nearly identical characteristic bands of ZIF-67. The results indicate no obvious chemical interactions among ZIF-67, Fc and BPNSs.
Chemical compositions of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 were characterized by XPS spectra. In Figure 2c, the full-scan XPS spectra indicate a series of characteristic peaks that are ascribed to the existence of surface elements, such as $\mathrm{Co2p}$ , O1s, N1s and C1s.50,51 The high-resolution XPS spectra of Co2p present fine characteristics of binding energies (Figure 2d). Two distinct shake-up bands at $797.5\mathrm{eV}$ and $781.5\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the Co $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ peaks, respectively. The results demonstrate the coexistence of two valence states, i.e. $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Co^{+}}$ . UV–vis absorption spectra were characterized. In Figure 2e, BPNSs show a wide absorption and a typical absorption peak appears at ${\sim}250~\mathrm{nm}$ .52 As for ZIF-67 before and after the doping of Fc or the drop-casting of BPNSs, the products display a characteristic absorption band ${\sim}600~\mathrm{nm}$ ) of tetrahedral-coordinated $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ , originating from the Co-MOF matrix of ZIF-67.53 Raman spectra of Fc/ZIF-67 and BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 are close to those of initial ZIF-67 without the doping or drop-casting process (Figure 2f). Typical Raman peak sites are assigned to vibrations of $\mathrm{C\mathrm{-}H}(3125,1455\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ , vibrations of symmetrical and asymmetrical methyl C–H ( $2935~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and vibration of C–N ( $1125~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ). Besides, several low-energy Raman bands $(<500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) come from metal-ligand vibrational modes, suggesting coordination interactions of 2-methylimidazole ligands with Co centers.51
Electrochemical Characterizations of Modified ITO. Modification process of ITO surface was characterized by measuring electrochemical curves. The bare and modified ITO electrodes were immersed into electrolyte solution containing deoxygenated PBS, KCl and $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4-}}$ as a redox probe. In Figure 3a, CV curves were measured under various modifications on ITO surface. In contrast to Fc/ZIF-67/ITO (curve c), BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO (curve d) has a higher cathodic peak current because of an improved redox of $[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]^{3-4-}$ probe. The results are due to synergistic effects between BPNSs and Fc/ZIF-67, so promoting the conductivity of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 hybrid.26,27 BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 hybrid thin-film on ITO enlarges effective surface areas and elevates charge transfer rates, which are conducive to sensitive redox responses and electrochemical signal outputs. By combining MB-labeled aptamer, the cathodic peak current of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO (curve e) is lower than that of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO. The combining action may partially block charge transfer during the redox process of $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4-}}$ probe on ITO surface.27 Interface properties of the modified ITO were characterized by EIS curves (Figure 3b). Charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ depends on electron transfer kinetics of redox probe at electrode interface. $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ is calculated through semicircle diameters of the Nyquist diagram. After modifications on ITO surface, large semicircle domains appear and indicate resistance action of charge transfer. $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO is lower than that of Fc/ZIF-67/ITO, while $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO is higher than that of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO. As a couple of reciprocal parameters (redox current, $R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , their change trends are opposite reasonably. High agreement between CV and EIS curves confirm the construction of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO platform.
Figure 3. (a) CV and (b) EIS curves measured from bare ITO a) and ITO modified with ZIF-67 b), Fc/ZIF-67 c), BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 d) and aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 e). ITO electrodes were immersed in electrolyte solution containing PBS (1 mM, pH 7.4), 0.1 M KCl and $\mathbf{l}\ \mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]^{3-4-}$ . (c) SWV curves of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 prepared at different contents of MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer $(0.2\mathrm{-}1.0~\upmu\mathrm{{M})}$ . (d) Peak current of MB and adsorption efficiency changes at different aptamer contents.
Optimization of Conditions for Electrochemical Measurements. As for aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO sensing platform, SWV curves were measured at different aptamer contents, incubation temperatures, times and pH values, aiming to study the optimal conditions for electrochemical measurements. With increase of aptamer contents, SWV curves of the platform were measured. In Figure 3c, the redox peak current of MB $\mathbf{(I_{MB})}$ apparently increases and reaches the maximum at $0.8~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of aptamer. A further increase of aptamer contents only leads to slight changes of ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ . These results reveal that the coupling interactions of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 hybrid with MB-labeled aptamer reach the saturation of aptamer adsorption on ITO (Figure 3d). The surface of BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 is almost fully occupied by the maximal aptamer and cannot adsorb excessive aptamer due to the lack of surface binding sites.4,10 Hence, 0.8 $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of aptamer is the optimal content to construct the platform with superior electrochemical performance.
With each increment of incubation temperature, ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ reduces markedly and then shows a slight increase at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 4a). The minimal ${\bf I}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ appears at $37.4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is close to the case at physiological temperature of $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Upon incubation at $37.4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ has a maximized decrease after addition of the targeted exosomes (Figure 4b), indicating the maximal ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ responses of the platform on exosomes. In view of applications in biological samples, $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ serves as the optimal temperature for sample incubation. Upon incubation for different times at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ dramatically decreases and reaches the equilibrium at $45\ \mathrm{min}$ and $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Figure 4c). Incubation time extending to 90 min induces a further decrease of ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ . Much remarkable decrease of ${\bf I}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ happens after addition of exosomes and incubation for $45~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Figure 4d). To save experimental time and realize rapid detection of exosomes, $45~\mathrm{{min}}$ is used as the optimal incubation time. pH of electrolyte solution exhibits an obvious influence on SWV curves of the platform. With increase of $\mathfrak{p H}$ , ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ gradually reduces and then increases (Figure 4e). ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ reaches the minimum at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ , denoting a maximal change of ${\bf I}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ after addition of exosomes. Besides, the ratio of dual-signal intensities $(\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{MB}})$ reaches the maximum at $\mathfrak{p H7.4}$ (Figure 4f). Due to the doping of Fc into the Co-MOF matrix of ZIF-67, redox peak current of Fc $(\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{Fc}})$ suffers from negligible effects during the changes of external conditions. The optimal incubation temperature, time and $\mathfrak{p H}$ are $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 45 min and pH 7.4, respectively.
Fabrication Process of Intrinsic Self-Calibration Aptasensor of Exosomes. At optimal conditions, SWV curves of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO platform were measured in the presence of exosomes (Figure 5a). Upon increase of exosomes concentration ( $\mathbf{\nabla}(C_{\mathrm{exo}})$ , ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ decreases gradually, but ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}$ is almost unchanged. The specific coupling of MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer with exosomes generates a new aptamer-exosomes complex away from ITO surface. The coupling action is competitively stronger than that of the aptamer adhering to BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 thin-film on ITO via intermolecular actions, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions.26,27,54,55 Electroactive Fc is doped in the Co-MOF matrix of ZIF-67 and ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}$ has slight changes upon addition of exosomes.27 ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}$ and ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ act as reference and response signals, respectively. There is a well-plotted linear relationship $R^{2}=0.9916$ between ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ and logarithm of $C_{\mathrm{exo}}$ in the range from $1.3{\times}10^{2}$ to $2.6\times10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (Figure 5b). Limit of detection (LOD) of exosomes is ${\sim}100$ particles $\mathrm{\mL^{-1}}$ , calculated by signal-to-noise ( $S/N=3)$ . A novel dual-signal and intrinsic self-calibration aptasensor of exosomes was formed by the platform. To assess this aptasensor, its performances for exosomes detection were compared with previous methods, as summarized in Table S1 of Supporting Information (SI). By contrast, this aptasensor has unique dual-signal and intrinsic self-calibration functions during the detection of exosomes and has a lower LOD, revealing its capability for sensitive and accurate detection of low-concentration exosomes.
Selectivity Evaluation and Precise Protein Capture Capability of this Aptasensor. To assess the selectivity of this aptasensor for exosomes detection, aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO platform was immersed into PBS ( $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\mathcal~{~1~}~}\mathrm{mM}$ 。 $\mathrm{pH}7.4\AA)$ containing exosomes derived from different types of cancer cells. SWV curves were measured and ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ values were calculated. As shown in Figure 5c, $\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{MB}}\left(-1.30\right)$ measured from the addition of MCF-7 cell-derived exosomes is much higher than that from HepG2 and HeLa cell-derived exosomes $(\sim0.75)$ . The latter is close to the control without exosomes $(\sim0.70)$ . Exosomes derived from MCF-7 cells can produce the highest ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ , compared with those derived from HepG2 and HeLa cells. The results illustrate that CD63 protein expression of MCF-7 cellderived exosomes is higher than that of other cancer cell-derived exosomes.20–25,44 This aptasensor has an excellent capability to selectively recognize MCF-7 cell-derived exosomes, which is due to the specific combination between MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer and its targeted CD63 protein. The MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer with artificial base sequences has a high recognition capability of CD63 protein. The aptamer was used to prepare the platform that can specifically recognize CD63 protein. MCF-7 cell-derived exosomes possess high expression of CD63 protein. The aptamer on BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 hybrid thin-film enables precise capture of CD63 protein from MCF-7 cell-derived exosomes, facilitating selective detection of MCF-7 cell-derived exosomes.
To assess the precise protein capture capability of this aptasensor, we compared the performances of MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer (CD63 aptamer) and a random aptamer with random base sequences. Cell culture medium (DMEM supplemented with $10\%$ fetal bovine serum, $1\%$ penicillin/streptomycin), MCF-7 cell culture supernatant filtrate with $10\ \mathrm{kDa}$ filter and MCF-7 cell culture supernatant were used as different targets. The platform was immersed in PBS containing one type of targets. SWV curves were measured and ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ values were calculated. In Figure 5d, only cell culture supernatant (number 8) presents a remarkably high ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ , when CD63 aptamer was adopted to construct this platform. These results demonstrate that high ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ responses were produced during the recognition process of exosomes in cell culture supernatant.44 Specific recognition of CD63 aptamer towards exosomes in cell culture supernatant confirms high selectivity of this aptasensor.
Figure 5. (a) SWV curves of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO platform immersed into PBS ( $\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}_{1\mathrm{\mM}}$ , $\mathrm{pH}7.4\_$ ) with addition of exosomes at different concentrations from 0 to $2.6\times10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . (b) The linearly plotted relationship between ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ and $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ based on the data in (a). (c) ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ measured in PBS without (control) and with addition of exosomes derived from HepG2, HeLa and MCF-7 cells. (d) ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ from SWV curves of the platform prepared by using MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer (specific CD63 aptamer) or the random aptamer. Both platforms were immersed in PBS without (1, 5) and with addition of cell culture medium $(2,6)$ , cell culture supernatant filtrate (3, 7) and cell culture supernatant (4, 8). (e) ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ measured from SWV curves of the platform upon the presence of exosomes 1 $1.0{\times}10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) and each interference at $1~\mathrm{mM}$ . Current percentage $(\%)$ is expressed as $(\mathbf{I}_{0}-\mathbf{I})/\mathbf{I}_{0}\times100\%$ . ${\bf{I}}_{0}$ and I represent ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ measured before and after addition of exosomes and interferences. (f) Detection of $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ in plasma samples from three healthy individuals (1, 2, 3) and three breast cancer patients (4, 5, 6).
Detection of Specific Cancer-Derived Exosomes in Healthy Individuals and Patients. Before practical detection applications of this aptasensor, some interferences potentially coexisting in biological samples (human serum) were investigated by measuring SWV curves of aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67/ITO platform in the presence of exosomes and each interference. ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ serves as the indicator. In Figure 5e, the each interference at $1~\mathrm{mM}$ does not produce remarkable effects on ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ , with slight changes of current percentage $(3-9\%)$ . After addition of exosomes ( $1.0{\times}10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{mL^{-1}}$ ), there is a dramatic decrease of ${\bf I}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ (Figure 5a) and a markedly changed current percentage $(\sim87\%)$ . The results suggest high performance of this aptasensor for sensitive and selective detection of exosomes, against interferences in biological samples. To evaluate practicability, the detection assays of exosomes were performed in practical samples collected from healthy human serum.22 A series of PBS (1 mM, pH 7.4) with external addition of exosomes were prepared based on the standard addition method. SWV curves were measured through the platform. By using the plotted linear relationship between ${\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{Fc}}/{\mathbf{I}}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ and $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ (Figure 5b), $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ values of samples were detected. As summarized in Table S2 (SI), the detected values of $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ are close to the spiked ones, accompanied with high detection recoveries of $97.33{-}104.00\%$ and low relative standard deviations of $2.56{-}4.79\%$ . The results verify high feasibility and reliability of this aptasensor for efficient detection of exosomes in practical samples.
Furthermore, detection assays of exosomes were conducted in plasma samples collected from healthy individuals and breast cancer patients. The detailed procedures and detection methods are similar to the sections of exosomes detection in serum samples. $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ values of plasma samples were obtained by using the plotted linear relationship of ${\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{Fc}}}/{\bf{I}}_{\mathrm{{MB}}}$ versus $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ (Figure 5b). In Figure 5f, $c_{\mathrm{exo}}$ values in plasma of breast cancer patients are remarkable higher, compared with healthy individuals. The results are due to two main reasons. One is high expression level of CD63 protein on cancer cell-derived exosomes, compared with normal cells.8,9,44 As for the other, exosomes were secreted by specific breast cancer (MCF-7) cells, and thus this aptasensor enables highly selective and selective detection of specific cancer cell-derived exosomes. This aptasensor of exosomes can be expanded to multiple biomarkers from cell line exosomes and perform efficient detection of cancer-derived exosomes, which are beneficial for the early cancer screening and diagnosis of potential patients in clinical.
## ■ CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we developed a unique dual-signal and intrinsic self-calibration aptasensor of exosomes based on aptamer-BPNSs/Fc/ZIF-67 functional hybrid thin-film platform. The platform was constructed by facile assembly of BPNSs and Fc-doped Co-based MOF (Fc/ZIF-67) on ITO, followed by combining MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer on the hybrid thin-film surface. A novel aptasensor of exosomes was fabricated and can efficiently detect exosomes in the concentration range from $1.3{\times}10^{2}$ to $2.6\times10^{5}$ particles $\mathrm{\mL^{-1}}$ , with a low LOD of 100 particles $\mathrm{\mL^{-1}}$ . The aptasensor has excellent selectivity and sensitivity for detection of exosomes against interferences, and was used to detect exosomes in practical human serum samples, showing high detection recoveries and low relative standard deviations. Experimental results testify high selectivity of the aptasensor for the detection of MCF-7 cell-derived exosomes with high expression level of CD63 protein, over exosomes derived from other types of cancer cells. MB-labeled ssDNA aptamer from the platform enables specific recognition of CD63 protein with high expression level on MCF-7 cells, indicating the precise protein capture capability. Moreover, the aptasensor was used to detect exosomes in plasma samples of healthy individuals and breast cancer patients, revealing its significant potential for detection of specific cancer cell-derived exosomes. The aptasensor can be expanded to multiple biomarkers from cell line exosomes. Further studies will focus on the precise and facile detection of exosomes derived from various types of cancer cells, promoting the development of early cancer screening and diagnosis techniques.
